182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Climate change is already transforming our cities. Rising temperatures, extreme rainfall, prolonged droughts and declining biodiversity are putting increasing pressure on urban nature and the quality of life of residents. Reducing emissions is essential. But mitigation alone is not enough. We must also adapt our cities to a changing climate, restore biodiversity and make our neighbourhoods greener, cooler and more resilient.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Yet we face two major challenges:)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (1. We lack sufficient local data.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Climate change affects every neighbourhood differently. But we still lack the detailed, long-term observations needed to understand what is happening at street level, how urban nature is responding and which local interventions can make a difference.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (2. We need everyone to participate.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Governments and scientists cannot address these challenges alone. Lasting change requires the active involvement of residents, schools, communities and businesses. Yet many people lack accessible tools, knowledge and opportunities to contribute meaningfully.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
